practice.a:
Multiply by the conjugate to simplify:
$\displaystyle \sqrt{x^2+3x}-x=\frac{(\sqrt{x^2+3x}-x)(\sqrt{x^2+3x}+x)}{\sqrt{x^2+3x}+x}
=\frac{x^2+3x-x^2}{\sqrt{x^2+3x}+x}
=\frac{3x}{\sqrt{x^2+3x}+x}$.
Factor $x$ from the square root: $\sqrt{x^2+3x}=x\sqrt{1+\frac{3}{x}}$.
Thus the expression becomes $\displaystyle \frac{3x}{x\sqrt{1+\frac{3}{x}}+x}
=\frac{3}{\sqrt{1+\frac{3}{x}}+1}$.
As $x\to\infty$, $\frac{3}{x}\to0$, so $\sqrt{1+\frac{3}{x}}\to1$.
Therefore the limit is $\displaystyle \frac{3}{1+1}= \frac{3}{2}$.
